1
00:00:01,778 --> 00:00:13,485
[SPEAKER_00]: You mentioned that the sense of I am is real as such, in one of the past talks, but simply a mistaken identity.

2
00:00:15,191 --> 00:00:15,952
[SPEAKER_00]: If I got that right.

3
00:00:16,692 --> 00:00:32,925
[SPEAKER_00]: But when I try to find this real sense of I am, to properly identify it, I only find shifting feelings, thoughts and perceptions, leaving me confused about what exactly is real about my identity, if I can't locate it anywhere.

4
00:00:37,209 --> 00:00:37,569
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

5
00:00:41,492 --> 00:00:44,094
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess the answer to that is just on the same level.

6
00:00:45,455 --> 00:00:46,936
[SPEAKER_01]: What is real about your eyes?

7
00:00:48,356 --> 00:00:49,737
[SPEAKER_00]: What is real about your ears?

8
00:00:50,938 --> 00:00:52,479
[SPEAKER_01]: What is real about your sense organs?

9
00:00:52,619 --> 00:00:54,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Because none of them can experience itself.

10
00:00:57,903 --> 00:01:03,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, there is sight, there is sound, there is smell, there is tactile.

11
00:01:06,798 --> 00:01:15,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the expectation that real things are only the ones that I can make into sense objects, well, that's one of the deeply rooted wrong views.

12
00:01:17,801 --> 00:01:23,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Because... what is that question even referring to then?

13
00:01:26,403 --> 00:01:32,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, whoever asked that, obviously is referring to their own sense of self, that they have some recognition of it.

14
00:01:33,443 --> 00:01:34,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, they want it to be more.

15
00:01:35,224 --> 00:01:37,185
[SPEAKER_01]: They want to make it more palpable.

16
00:01:37,345 --> 00:01:39,106
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the wrong views, expectations.

17
00:01:39,446 --> 00:01:40,247
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to measure it.

18
00:01:40,327 --> 00:01:42,548
[SPEAKER_01]: They want to examine it as a sense object.

19
00:01:43,169 --> 00:01:49,152
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is that they were looking for something, means there was some perception of the sense of self, sense of I am.

20
00:01:52,323 --> 00:02:00,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Because otherwise, like, the entire question and the words and terminology used would be completely unintelligible if you don't even know what it refers to.

21
00:02:00,847 --> 00:02:03,468
[SPEAKER_01]: But they obviously have some sense of I am.

22
00:02:05,329 --> 00:02:07,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, what do they know what they're trying to find?

23
00:02:10,032 --> 00:02:10,672
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the point.

24
00:02:10,832 --> 00:02:16,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of being preoccupied in trying to find it, recognize the sense is already there, peripherally.

25
00:02:18,351 --> 00:02:23,235
[SPEAKER_01]: As something that can never be directly examined, yet it always exists in the background.

26
00:02:24,216 --> 00:02:25,237
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just like the eye.

27
00:02:25,757 --> 00:02:27,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Just like the eye, just like the ears.

28
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:34,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Because people get confused, because you can touch your eye and you think that's the eye that sees, but it's not.

29
00:02:35,765 --> 00:02:42,211
[SPEAKER_01]: The internal sense base, the eye that cannot see itself, is inaccessible to you.

30
00:02:43,215 --> 00:02:52,683
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how much you touch it, how much you look at the picture of it, how much you stare in the mirror, what you're seeing is the sight, not the thing because of which the sight is there.

31
00:02:55,605 --> 00:02:59,588
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why when the Buddha described the internal senses, similarly it's like the empty villages.

32
00:03:00,689 --> 00:03:04,833
[SPEAKER_01]: They get raided, defined by basically that which they're not.

33
00:03:08,896 --> 00:03:10,237
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's the same with the sense of self.

34
00:03:12,807 --> 00:03:31,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of following the natural expectation of finding it as a palpable, measurable thing, object of your senses, or of your thought, put that tendency aside, don't follow it, and instead ask yourself, well, what am I even trying to find?

35
00:03:32,037 --> 00:03:32,998
[SPEAKER_01]: That vague sense.

36
00:03:33,158 --> 00:03:39,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I obviously have some recognition of its vagueness, otherwise I wouldn't, as I said, I wouldn't even set off searching for it.

37
00:03:40,722 --> 00:03:46,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not just searching for an abstract idea that you don't understand what it refers to.

38
00:03:48,825 --> 00:03:51,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were, then why are you even looking for it?

39
00:03:51,986 --> 00:03:56,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, if it's an abstract idea, you hear about sense of self, you have absolutely no idea what the sense of I am means.

40
00:03:56,448 --> 00:03:57,268
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no reference.

41
00:03:57,328 --> 00:03:58,508
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it doesn't exist for you.

42
00:04:00,089 --> 00:04:02,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you be looking for it on the level of the sense objects?

43
00:04:04,053 --> 00:04:07,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you be questioning its realness, because it doesn't appear as much as...

44
00:04:07,555 --> 00:04:11,198
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you already kind of know that it's always there in the background.

45
00:04:11,618 --> 00:04:14,600
[SPEAKER_01]: It's felt ambiguous, not as palpable.

46
00:04:15,261 --> 00:04:22,186
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want it to be more so, because, again, that's what you deem as real, that's what you deem as necessary.

47
00:04:23,887 --> 00:04:25,468
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why you'll never get it that way.

48
00:04:30,016 --> 00:04:43,564
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like that, you know, the old simile I used to give, like, when you look at the stars at the night, and if you pick a bright star and stare at it, don't let your eyes look elsewhere, it's like thousands of peripheral little stars will appear.

49
00:04:43,644 --> 00:04:48,827
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you try to look at any of them, you just see blackness, because they can only be seen with peripheral vision.

50
00:04:51,249 --> 00:04:56,192
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't accept that, and you keep staring, you're just going to meet more and more frustration, because, again,

51
00:04:58,387 --> 00:05:05,070
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, one could come up with a view that, well, then, the sense of self doesn't exist, because I cannot directly find it.

52
00:05:05,451 --> 00:05:08,912
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is it that doesn't exist, then, where they refer to, if it doesn't exist?

53
00:05:09,073 --> 00:05:11,634
[SPEAKER_01]: If it truly doesn't exist, what are you referring to as non-existent?

54
00:05:12,494 --> 00:05:13,595
[SPEAKER_00]: That thing that you are denying.

55
00:05:13,895 --> 00:05:17,017
[SPEAKER_01]: You could more accurately say it doesn't exist as much.

56
00:05:18,277 --> 00:05:22,399
[SPEAKER_01]: As much to the layers, to the levels, to the extents that I deem existence to be.

57
00:05:22,540 --> 00:05:24,801
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that I can think it,

58
00:05:25,725 --> 00:05:30,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Peripherally, the fact that I can deny it, means that has to be there.

59
00:05:31,949 --> 00:05:33,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Something has to be there.

60
00:05:37,992 --> 00:05:39,713
[SPEAKER_00]: So what can we say that something is?

61
00:05:40,814 --> 00:05:42,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, again, you fall back into the same question.

62
00:05:43,055 --> 00:05:52,541
[SPEAKER_01]: What could I say that something is, means how could I make that something to be more than it is, because I don't like the ambiguity of it, and my mind is not used to seeing it as such.

63
00:05:52,861 --> 00:05:54,562
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to make it more palpable.

64
00:05:57,163 --> 00:05:58,364
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any question, what is it?

65
00:05:58,464 --> 00:05:58,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it?

66
00:05:58,784 --> 00:05:59,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it not?

67
00:05:59,524 --> 00:06:00,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it both?

68
00:06:00,224 --> 00:06:00,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it not?

69
00:06:01,625 --> 00:06:05,987
[SPEAKER_01]: It revolves around, come on, make it more than it is, so that I can deal with it.

70
00:06:06,807 --> 00:06:15,031
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha wouldn't answer those questions, because he's just reinforcing the very kind of wrong view and the very wrong way of going about it.

71
00:06:18,612 --> 00:06:23,795
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that not quite as real sense of self is real as such.

72
00:06:24,838 --> 00:06:25,558
[SPEAKER_01]: To that extent.

73
00:06:26,399 --> 00:06:32,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of trying to interfere or change or modify the extent, you need to learn how to see it impartially.

74
00:06:36,824 --> 00:06:40,606
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, that is a... you said that that is a sense of self.

75
00:06:42,547 --> 00:06:49,450
[SPEAKER_00]: The owner is a product or a symptom of this assumption of ownership.

76
00:06:50,010 --> 00:06:53,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, theoretically speaking, but practically that knowledge is irrelevant.

77
00:06:53,373 --> 00:07:00,398
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just gonna serve as an excuse and explanation of what my sense of self is, which is, again, done for the sake of making it more definable.

78
00:07:04,061 --> 00:07:04,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense of self is there.

79
00:07:06,162 --> 00:07:06,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Ownership is there.

80
00:07:07,383 --> 00:07:08,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot be accessed directly.

81
00:07:10,866 --> 00:07:11,887
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always in the background.

82
00:07:12,087 --> 00:07:13,007
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always peripheral.

83
00:07:14,028 --> 00:07:16,670
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's a symptom of the problem.

84
00:07:16,770 --> 00:07:17,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

85
00:07:18,892 --> 00:07:19,312
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like...

86
00:07:20,663 --> 00:07:23,766
[SPEAKER_01]: The heat is there because there is obviously fire.

87
00:07:24,166 --> 00:07:27,469
[SPEAKER_00]: And the fire is there because the fuel is constantly provided for it.

88
00:07:28,770 --> 00:07:30,472
[SPEAKER_01]: So if you try to get rid of the heat...

89
00:07:32,772 --> 00:07:36,976
[SPEAKER_01]: While still feeding the fire, well, it's a very futile endeavor.

90
00:07:38,297 --> 00:07:40,178
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's pretty much what people are doing.

91
00:07:40,459 --> 00:07:41,339
[SPEAKER_01]: They...

92
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:43,121
[SPEAKER_01]: I try to deal with heat, i.e.

93
00:07:43,161 --> 00:07:43,962
[SPEAKER_01]: with the sense of self.

94
00:07:44,022 --> 00:07:45,843
[SPEAKER_01]: I try to do this, I do that, it's anātā.

95
00:07:46,504 --> 00:07:52,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Repeat anātā mantras, and deny it, and say it's not real, and science has explained that everything is constantly changing, da-da-da-da-da.

96
00:07:52,789 --> 00:07:56,713
[SPEAKER_01]: While still inadvertently and ignorantly continuing to fuel the very fuel,

97
00:07:58,814 --> 00:08:01,536
[SPEAKER_01]: The very fire because of which the heat, it's still going to be there.

98
00:08:01,616 --> 00:08:05,279
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how much you deny it as an afterthought, it's still going to be felt.

99
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:06,920
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still going to burn.

100
00:08:08,501 --> 00:08:11,304
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you recognize, yeah, this heat is a bit too hot.

101
00:08:11,344 --> 00:08:12,624
[SPEAKER_01]: It's burning.

102
00:08:12,645 --> 00:08:13,125
[SPEAKER_01]: It's too much.

103
00:08:13,705 --> 00:08:14,266
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a problem.

104
00:08:15,907 --> 00:08:16,848
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do I get rid of it?

105
00:08:16,868 --> 00:08:20,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by extinguishing the underlying fire.

106
00:08:20,710 --> 00:08:22,632
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is that underlying fire?

107
00:08:22,832 --> 00:08:25,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Actions rooted in greed, aversion, distraction.

108
00:08:26,547 --> 00:08:34,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance, resistance, hindrances, denial, unbeneficial, basically, acts by body, speech and mind.

109
00:08:35,053 --> 00:08:35,673
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the fire.

110
00:08:36,114 --> 00:08:37,114
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the fuel for the fire.

111
00:08:40,096 --> 00:08:45,120
[SPEAKER_01]: That's also why you have to train in it, to see it, and to get benefits from it.

112
00:08:46,021 --> 00:08:50,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Because just providing an answer will not immediately extinguish the fire.

113
00:08:50,984 --> 00:08:55,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even just, like, not providing the fuel for a day or two,

114
00:08:56,257 --> 00:08:59,219
[SPEAKER_01]: will not immediately show you that the fire is lessening.

115
00:08:59,299 --> 00:08:59,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

116
00:08:59,640 --> 00:09:01,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because it was overloaded with fuel before that.

117
00:09:03,042 --> 00:09:11,368
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is a bit of a delay that you have to, as I say, endure until you recognize that the fire is lessening and the heat is diminishing.

118
00:09:16,632 --> 00:09:18,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the fire is out and then the fire is out.

119
00:09:18,534 --> 00:09:19,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Eventually, it will be out, of course.

120
00:09:24,604 --> 00:09:28,987
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can have a view that the fire can go out.

121
00:09:29,027 --> 00:09:29,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, magically, yeah.

122
00:09:30,148 --> 00:09:32,830
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why I say the sense of self is real as such.

123
00:09:33,190 --> 00:09:36,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Ambiguous, peripheral, not directly accessible, but it is real as such.

124
00:09:37,493 --> 00:09:39,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the heat is real as such.

125
00:09:40,956 --> 00:09:46,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Not seeing the heat as an indirect by-product of an underlying problem,

126
00:09:47,388 --> 00:09:50,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Will exactly make you go about it the way people go about it.

127
00:09:50,410 --> 00:09:56,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to make the sense of self into something measurable, something deniable, something that's not real, something that's an illusion.

128
00:09:56,673 --> 00:09:59,695
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, just denying the heat because you can't find the fire.

129
00:10:02,036 --> 00:10:08,420
[SPEAKER_01]: And then just trying to cope by saying, well, you know, it's not real anyway and what is it anyway when I can't?

130
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:09,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't.

131
00:10:09,100 --> 00:10:09,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you touch the heat?

132
00:10:11,001 --> 00:10:11,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you see the heat?

133
00:10:13,865 --> 00:10:14,005
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

134
00:10:14,666 --> 00:10:15,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it's there.

135
00:10:15,886 --> 00:10:16,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Felt.

136
00:10:16,787 --> 00:10:17,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Burning.

137
00:10:17,568 --> 00:10:18,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressuring.

138
00:10:25,373 --> 00:10:26,714
[SPEAKER_00]: The fueling is palpable.

139
00:10:27,074 --> 00:10:28,055
[SPEAKER_00]: You can, you know...

140
00:10:28,235 --> 00:10:31,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, if you make the right associations.

141
00:10:32,477 --> 00:10:33,898
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not going to be seen on its own.

142
00:10:34,018 --> 00:10:43,545
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not going to make the connection between seemingly random acts of desire, irritation and stuff, and then subsequent heat that pressures you in regard to completely different things.

143
00:10:45,253 --> 00:10:46,994
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why the learning is necessary.

144
00:10:47,074 --> 00:10:51,217
[SPEAKER_01]: So you start to look for the right connections and putting two and two together.

145
00:10:54,819 --> 00:11:00,862
[SPEAKER_01]: And initially, yeah, you might even have to take it on faith that certain acts are increasing the heat, but you don't see the connection well.

146
00:11:01,623 --> 00:11:03,204
[SPEAKER_01]: You have nothing to lose if you take it on faith.

147
00:11:04,364 --> 00:11:08,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the actions that are required from you to cultivate are beneficial even in the worldly sense.

148
00:11:10,140 --> 00:11:11,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't follow the desires blindly.

149
00:11:11,560 --> 00:11:14,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't act out of anger by body, speech and mind.

150
00:11:14,842 --> 00:11:16,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't entertain such thoughts.

151
00:11:16,362 --> 00:11:18,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, well, that's a good thing anyway, isn't it?

152
00:11:18,403 --> 00:11:19,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody values that.

153
00:11:19,283 --> 00:11:20,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Even people have no interest in dhamma.

154
00:11:21,424 --> 00:11:22,444
[SPEAKER_01]: If you ask them, is anger bad?

155
00:11:22,464 --> 00:11:23,344
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, yeah.

156
00:11:23,384 --> 00:11:24,084
[SPEAKER_01]: How is anger felt?

157
00:11:24,265 --> 00:11:25,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasantly or unpleasantly?

158
00:11:25,125 --> 00:11:25,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's unpleasant.

159
00:11:28,506 --> 00:11:31,227
[SPEAKER_01]: How is desire felt?

160
00:11:31,267 --> 00:11:33,948
[SPEAKER_01]: If you can't get what you want, but desire remains there, highlighted.

161
00:11:34,088 --> 00:11:34,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's unpleasant.

162
00:11:35,729 --> 00:11:37,611
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it would be good if you weren't there.

163
00:11:37,631 --> 00:11:40,093
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be good if you would be in a bit more control in regard to those things.

164
00:11:40,113 --> 00:11:40,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it would be nice, it would be...

165
00:11:41,675 --> 00:11:42,676
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be peaceful, wouldn't it?

166
00:11:42,916 --> 00:11:45,158
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind would be quieter, the mind would be more content.

167
00:11:46,459 --> 00:11:48,601
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have nothing to lose, even if you take it in faith.

168
00:11:49,642 --> 00:11:54,187
[SPEAKER_01]: That those kind of behaviors and not following those types of other unbeneficial behaviors is good.

169
00:11:54,888 --> 00:12:00,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Plus, as the Buddha said in his instructions, it's... as a result of those things, the heat will lessen.

170
00:12:01,987 --> 00:12:12,529
[SPEAKER_01]: As a result of those things, your own kind of need and compulsion for things to be clearly palpable, more defined, to be real, that will diminish as well.

171
00:12:12,669 --> 00:12:13,549
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize that.

172
00:12:14,269 --> 00:12:15,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, look at that thing.

173
00:12:15,070 --> 00:12:15,750
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not as real.

174
00:12:16,010 --> 00:12:16,930
[SPEAKER_01]: That is real as such.

175
00:12:17,730 --> 00:12:18,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, look at that thing.

176
00:12:18,410 --> 00:12:18,770
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not...

177
00:12:19,150 --> 00:12:20,451
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't put my finger on it.

178
00:12:20,851 --> 00:12:21,611
[SPEAKER_01]: It is real as such.

179
00:12:21,711 --> 00:12:25,172
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of being unable to put a finger on something is real as such.

180
00:12:25,572 --> 00:12:26,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Ambiguity.

181
00:12:27,152 --> 00:12:27,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncertainty.

182
00:12:28,272 --> 00:12:29,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Real as such.

183
00:12:29,392 --> 00:12:31,873
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't need to change it in order to deal with it.

184
00:12:32,595 --> 00:12:35,338
[SPEAKER_00]: You see it for what it is.

185
00:12:35,358 --> 00:12:38,682
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you go over doubt as well, overcome the doubt, the uncertainty.

186
00:12:41,005 --> 00:12:48,533
[SPEAKER_01]: You overcome the doubt by recognizing uncertainty real as such, and not feeling justified in automatically trying to get rid of it or explain in a way.

